lue":" is the\nempty pixel distance between neighboring circle edges and defaults to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"1"},{"type":"text","value":".\nIt must be finite and nonnegative."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fit"},{"type":"text","value":" defaults to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"overflow"},{"type":"text","value":", preserving collision-free placement even when\nthe swarm extends beyond the plot. Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fit: 'compress'"},{"type":"text","value":" to fit crowded\noffsets inside the plot without changing the measured coordinate or radii.\nCompression can make circles overlap. Layouts that already fit are unchanged.\nIf a circle's diameter exceeds the available plot dimension, compression\nthrows a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RangeError"},{"type":"text","value":"; reduce "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"r"},{"type":"text","value":" or provide more space. Clipping an overflowing\nswarm does not remove its points from keyboard or pointer interaction."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The public type surface also includes "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"CreateDotLayoutOptions"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"DotLayout"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"DotLayoutResolveContext"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"DodgeOptions"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"DodgeXAnchor"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"DodgeYAnchor"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"DodgeXOptions"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"DodgeYOptions"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"custom-layouts","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom layouts"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createDotLayout"},{"type":"text","value":" when placement depends on final plot bounds but is not a\ndodge. The resolver receives the preserved channel's scaled pixel positions\nand the final radii in materialized valid-row order. Return one finite\ncross-axis pixel position per materialized row. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dot"},{"type":"text","value":" validates the result and\nretains the authored "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"anchor"},{"type":"text","value":" as the derived interaction-point value."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const rowLayout = createDotLayout({\n  axis: 'y',\n  anchor: 'rows',\n  resolve: ({ chart, measuredPositions, radii }) =>\n    measuredPositions.map(\n      (_position, index) => chart.y + radii[index] + index * 12,\n    ),\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"scale-ownership","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scale ownership"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The measured channel remains an ordinary semantic chart value. A "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dodgeY"},{"type":"text","value":"\ndot contributes only x scale values, so its chart definition needs an x scale\nbut no y scale. A "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dodgeX"},{"type":"text","value":" dot needs only a y scale."}]},{"type":"code","value":"defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    dot(rows, {\n      x: 'economy',\n      layout: dodgeY({ anchor: 'middle' }),\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: scaleLinear().domain([5, 50]) },\n    y: null,\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Do not configure the generated channel: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y"},{"type":"text","value":" with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dodgeY"},{"type":"text","value":", or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x"},{"type":"text","value":" with\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dodgeX"},{"type":"text","value":". The generated interaction-point value is the logical anchor while\nits "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y"},{"type":"text","value":" fields contain the actual laid-out pixel center."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"collision-and-radius","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Collision and radius"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Collision distance is the sum of both final dot radii and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"padding"},{"type":"text","value":". Configure\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"r"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rScale"},{"type":"text","value":" once on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dot"},{"type":"text","value":"; the layout receives the same resolved radii\nused for rendering."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Placement is synchronous and deterministic. Edge anchors choose the nearest\nvalid inward position. Middle anchors choose the valid position with the\nsmallest absolute displacement. Fixed-radius rows retain source order;\nvariable-radius rows are placed largest first with source order as the stable\ntie-breaker."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"identity-facets-and-grouping","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Identity, facets, and grouping"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every rendered point retains its source datum, source index, key, group,\nstate, and motion policy. Supply "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"key"},{"type":"text","value":" when the measured channel contains\nduplicates."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each facet resolves its child dodge layout against that cell's final scales\nand bounds. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"color"},{"type":"text","value":" affect ordinary dot grouping and paint; they do not\ncreate separate collision lanes. Use facets when groups need independent\nswarm bounds."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"signatures","text":"Signatures","level":2},{"id":"custom-layouts","text":"Custom layouts","level":2},{"id":"scale-ownership","text":"Scale ownership","level":2},{"id":"collision-and-radius","text":"Collision and radius","level":2},{"id":"identity-facets-and-grouping","text":"Identity, facets, and grouping","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\n`dodgeY` preserves each dot's scaled x position and derives a collision-free y\nposition in final plot pixels. `dodgeX` transposes the layout: y is preserved\nand x is derived.\n\n```ts\nimport { dodgeY } from '@tanstack/charts/dodge'\nimport { dot } from '@tanstack/charts/dot'\n\ndot(rows, {\n  x: 'value',\n  key: 'id',\n  r: 4,\n  layout: dodgeY({\n    anchor: 'middle',\n    padding: 1,\n  }),\n})\n```\n\nThe layouts and `createDotLayout` are also exported from `@tanstack/charts` and\n`@tanstack/charts/universal`.\n\n## Signatures\n\n```ts\nfunction dodgeY(options?: {\n  anchor?: 'top' | 'middle' | 'bottom'\n  padding?: number\n  fit?: 'overflow' | 'compress'\n}): DodgeYLayout\n\nfunction dodgeX(options?: {\n  anchor?: 'left' | 'middle' | 'right'\n  padding?: number\n  fit?: 'overflow' | 'compress'\n}): DodgeXLayout\n\nfunction createDotLayout(options: {\n  axis: 'x' | 'y'\n  anchor: ChartValue\n  resolve(context: {\n    chart: ChartBounds\n    measuredPositions: readonly number[]\n    radii: readonly number[]\n  }): readonly number[]\n}): DotLayout\n```\n\n`dodgeY` defaults to `bottom`; `dodgeX` defaults to `left`. `padding` is the\nempty pixel distance between neighboring circle edges and defaults to `1`.\nIt must be finite and nonnegative.\n\n`fit` defaults to `overflow`, preserving collision-free placement even when\nthe swarm extends beyond the plot. Use `fit: 'compress'` to fit crowded\noffsets inside the plot without changing the measured coordinate or radii.\nCompression can make circles overlap. Layouts that already fit are unchanged.\nIf a circle's diameter exceeds the available plot dimension, compression\nthrows a `RangeError`; reduce `r` or provide more space. Clipping an overflowing\nswarm does not remove its points from keyboard or pointer interaction.\n\nThe public type surface also includes `CreateDotLayoutOptions`, `DotLayout`,\n`DotLayoutResolveContext`, `DodgeOptions`, `DodgeXAnchor`, `DodgeYAnchor`,\n`DodgeXOptions`, and `DodgeYOptions`.\n\n## Custom layouts\n\nUse `createDotLayout` when placement depends on final plot bounds but is not a\ndodge. The resolver receives the preserved channel's scaled pixel positions\nand the final radii in materialized valid-row order. Return one finite\ncross-axis pixel position per materialized row. `dot` validates the result and\nretains the authored `anchor` as the derived interaction-point value.\n\n```ts\nconst rowLayout = createDotLayout({\n  axis: 'y',\n  anchor: 'rows',\n  resolve: ({ chart, measuredPositions, radii }) =>\n    measuredPositions.map(\n      (_position, index) => chart.y + radii[index] + index * 12,\n    ),\n})\n```\n\n## Scale ownership\n\nThe measured channel remains an ordinary semantic chart value. A `dodgeY`\ndot contributes only x scale values, so its chart definition needs an x scale\nbut no y scale. A `dodgeX` dot needs only a y scale.\n\n```ts\ndefineChart({\n  marks: [\n    dot(rows, {\n      x: 'economy',\n      layout: dodgeY({ anchor: 'middle' }),\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: scaleLinear().domain([5, 50]) },\n    y: null,\n  },\n})\n```\n\nDo not configure the generated channel: `y` with `dodgeY`, or `x` with\n`dodgeX`. The generated interaction-point value is the logical anchor while\nits `x` and `y` fields contain the actual laid-out pixel center.\n\n## Collision and radius\n\nCollision distance is the sum of both final dot radii and `padding`. Configure\n`r` and `rScale` once on `dot`; the layout receives the same resolved radii\nused for rendering.\n\nPlacement is synchronous and deterministic. Edge anchors choose the nearest\nvalid inward position. Middle anchors choose the valid position with the\nsmallest absolute displacement. Fixed-radius rows retain source order;\nvariable-radius rows are placed largest first with source order as the stable\ntie-breaker.\n\n## Identity, facets, and grouping\n\nEvery rendered point retains its source datum, source index, key, group,\nstate, and motion policy. Supply `key` when the measured channel contains\nduplicates.\n\nEach facet resolves its child dodge layout against that cell's final scales\nand bounds. `z` and `color` affect ordinary dot grouping and paint; they do not\ncreate separate collision lanes. Use facets when groups need independent\nswarm bounds.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Dodge Layouts\ndescription: Reference for responsive dodgeX and dodgeY dot collision layouts, anchors, padding, radii, identity, and scale ownership.\n---\n\n`dodgeY` preserves each dot's scaled x position and derives a collision-free y\nposition in final plot pixels. `dodgeX` transposes the layout: y is preserved\nand x is derived.\n\n```ts\nimport { dodgeY } from '@tanstack/charts/dodge'\nimport { dot } from '@tanstack/charts/dot'\n\ndot(rows, {\n  x: 'value',\n  key: 'id',\n  r: 4,\n  layout: dodgeY({\n    anchor: 'middle',\n    padding: 1,\n  }),\n})\n```\n\nThe layouts and `createDotLayout` are also exported from `@tanstack/charts` and\n`@tanstack/charts/universal`.\n\n## Signatures\n\n```ts\nfunction dodgeY(options?: {\n  anchor?: 'top' | 'middle' | 'bottom'\n  padding?: number\n  fit?: 'overflow' | 'compress'\n}): DodgeYLayout\n\nfunction dodgeX(options?: {\n  anchor?: 'left' | 'middle' | 'right'\n  padding?: number\n  fit?: 'overflow' | 'compress'\n}): DodgeXLayout\n\nfunction createDotLayout(options: {\n  axis: 'x' | 'y'\n  anchor: ChartValue\n  resolve(context: {\n    chart: ChartBounds\n    measuredPositions: readonly number[]\n    radii: readonly number[]\n  }): readonly number[]\n}): DotLayout\n```\n\n`dodgeY` defaults to `bottom`; `dodgeX` defaults to `left`. `padding` is the\nempty pixel distance between neighboring circle edges and defaults to `1`.\nIt must be finite and nonnegative.\n\n`fit` defaults to `overflow`, preserving collision-free placement even when\nthe swarm extends beyond the plot. Use `fit: 'compress'` to fit crowded\noffsets inside the plot without changing the measured coordinate or radii.\nCompression can make circles overlap. Layouts that already fit are unchanged.\nIf a circle's diameter exceeds the available plot dimension, compression\nthrows a `RangeError`; reduce `r` or provide more space. Clipping an overflowing\nswarm does not remove its points from keyboard or pointer interaction.\n\nThe public type surface also includes `CreateDotLayoutOptions`, `DotLayout`,\n`DotLayoutResolveContext`, `DodgeOptions`, `DodgeXAnchor`, `DodgeYAnchor`,\n`DodgeXOptions`, and `DodgeYOptions`.\n\n## Custom layouts\n\nUse `createDotLayout` when placement depends on final plot bounds but is not a\ndodge. The resolver receives the preserved channel's scaled pixel positions\nand the final radii in materialized valid-row order. Return one finite\ncross-axis pixel position per materialized row. `dot` validates the result and\nretains the authored `anchor` as the derived interaction-point value.\n\n```ts\nconst rowLayout = createDotLayout({\n  axis: 'y',\n  anchor: 'rows',\n  resolve: ({ chart, measuredPositions, radii }) =>\n    measuredPositions.map(\n      (_position, index) => chart.y + radii[index] + index * 12,\n    ),\n})\n```\n\n## Scale ownership\n\nThe measured channel remains an ordinary semantic chart value. A `dodgeY`\ndot contributes only x scale values, so its chart definition needs an x scale\nbut no y scale. A `dodgeX` dot needs only a y scale.\n\n```ts\ndefineChart({\n  marks: [\n    dot(rows, {\n      x: 'economy',\n      layout: dodgeY({ anchor: 'middle' }),\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: scaleLinear().domain([5, 50]) },\n    y: null,\n  },\n})\n```\n\nDo not configure the generated channel: `y` with `dodgeY`, or `x` with\n`dodgeX`. The generated interaction-point value is the logical anchor while\nits `x` and `y` fields contain the actual laid-out pixel center.\n\n## Collision and radius\n\nCollision distance is the sum of both final dot radii and `padding`. Configure\n`r` and `rScale` once on `dot`; the layout receives the same resolved radii\nused for rendering.\n\nPlacement is synchronous and deterministic. Edge anchors choose the nearest\nvalid inward position. Middle anchors choose the valid position with the\nsmallest absolute displacement. Fixed-radius rows retain source order;\nvariable-radius rows are placed largest first with source order as the stable\ntie-breaker.\n\n## Identity, facets, and grouping\n\nEvery rendered point retains its source datum, source index, key, group,\nstate, and motion policy. Supply `key` when the measured channel contains\nduplicates.\n\nEach facet resolves its child dodge layout against that cell's final scales\nand bounds. `z` and `color` affect ordinary dot grouping and paint; they do not\ncreate separate collision lanes. Use facets when groups need independent\nswarm bounds.\n"}
